{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "08", "chapter_title": "AI-Enhanced Presentation Development", "title": "Module 5 Presentation Studio", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 5 presentation studio to presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development", "cat100_supplement_module_5_presentation_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 8, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/05/07"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_calibration"]}, "content": "# Module 5 Presentation Studio\n\n![Module 5 Presentation Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-08-module-5-presentation-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe seven skills in this module are not a menu to pick from; they are a pipeline that runs in order, and skipping a stage breaks the ones downstream. If you choose a theme before you fix the slide structure, you protect a pretty layout instead of a clear message. If you check accessibility before you settle the content, you re-check it again after every edit. The capstone studio is where you stop practicing the moves in isolation and run the whole pipeline on a real deck, then attach a revision trail that proves you did.\n\nThis section gives you the assembly order and a single per-slide checklist that folds purpose, structure, media, narration, citation, and accessibility into one pass. The artifact is a complete storyboard with that revision trail, so a reviewer can inspect not just the finished deck but the decisions that produced it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student has a deck that looks done: nice theme, smooth animations, confident narration. Run it through the pipeline and the cracks appear. Slide 3's title is the label \"Results.\" The chart on slide 4 was \"found online.\" The status colors on slide 5 fail in grayscale. None of these were visible to someone admiring the finish, because polish hides structural gaps. The studio's job is to make you walk every slide through the same six checks so the gaps surface before the audience finds them.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThink of a deck as passing through six dependent layers, each building on the one before:\n\n| Layer | Question it answers | Section it comes from |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Purpose | Who is the audience and what action do they take? | 05.01 |\n| Structure | Does each title assert a claim the evidence proves? | 05.02 |\n| Media | Does each image explain (and is it verified)? | 05.03 |\n| Narration | Does the voice add reasoning, not reread the slide? | 05.04, 05.05 |\n| Citation | Is every source and figure verifiable, AI disclosed? | 05.06 |\n| Accessibility | Does it reach every viewer (contrast, alt text, not color-only)? | 05.07 |\n\nThe order is not arbitrary. Purpose decides which content belongs, so it comes first; there is no point structuring a slide the audience does not need. Structure decides what each slide claims, so media, narration, and citation can attach to a real assertion rather than a label. Accessibility comes last among the checks not because it matters least, but because it must be re-verified after content is final; checking contrast on a slide you are about to rewrite wastes the check.\n\nWhat separates the capstone from the individual sections is the **revision trail**. Anyone can produce a deck; a defensible submission shows the earlier version, the gap each layer exposed, the support you used, and at least one suggestion you rejected with the reason. The trail is the evidence that you ran the pipeline rather than just arriving at a clean-looking result.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: assemble the Module 5 capstone deck by walking the full pipeline, then attach a revision trail.\n\nPipeline checklist applied to one slide:\n\n| Layer | Check | Result on slide 3 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Purpose | Does it serve the hiring panel? | Yes, shows the analyst skill match |\n| Structure | Assertion headline? | Fixed: \"Results\" -> \"Cut report time 40%\" |\n| Media | Explanatory, verified chart? | Yes, with BLS 2025 source line |\n| Narration | Adds meaning, not reread? | Rewrote to give the cause |\n| Citation | Verifiable + AI disclosed? | Yes, link points to the exact table |\n| Accessibility | Contrast, alt text, not color-only? | Failed contrast, darkened title to pass |\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Run every slide through the six layers above; do not skip to polish.\n2. Fix the two gaps found (the label headline and the failed contrast).\n3. Attach a revision trail: the earlier slide 3, the contrast issue, the alt-text help used from BigAL, and one suggestion you rejected (an animated transition) with the reason it added no meaning.\n\nNotice that two of the six layers caught problems on a single slide that already looked finished: structure (a label title) and accessibility (failed contrast). A deck that \"looks done\" routinely fails two or three layers per slide, which is exactly why the pass has to be systematic rather than a final glance.\n\nWalk the capstone pipeline one decision at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run a finished-looking deck through the six layers\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"A deck has a nice theme, smooth animations, and confident narration. Where do you start the capstone pass?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"At polish; the surface is already strong\",\"At purpose: confirm who the audience is and what action the deck drives\",\"At accessibility, since it is the strictest check\",\"At narration, since the voice is already recorded\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Start at purpose. It decides which content belongs, so there is no point structuring a slide the audience does not need. The six layers run in dependency order.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Slide 3\u2019s title is the label \\\"Results\\\" and its contrast fails. Which layers caught these, and when do you fix contrast?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Polish caught both; fix them together at the end\",\"Structure caught the label title; accessibility caught contrast, fixed last once content is final\",\"Purpose caught both; fix before anything else\",\"Citation caught both; fix during sourcing\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Structure flags the label title (rewrite to \\\"Cut report time 40%\\\"); accessibility flags contrast. Fix contrast last, because re-checking it on a slide you are about to rewrite wastes the check.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Attach the revision trail: the earlier slide 3, the gap each layer exposed, the support you used (alt-text help from BigAL, disclosed), and one rejected suggestion (an animated transition) with the reason it added no meaning. The trail, not the finish, is the evidence.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student wants to check accessibility first because it feels like the most rigorous layer. Why does the pipeline put accessibility last?\" explanation=\"Accessibility must be re-verified after content is final. Checking contrast or labels on a slide you are about to rewrite means re-checking after every edit; running it last lands the check on stable content.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Accessibility matters least, so it goes last\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It must be re-verified after content is final, so checking it early forces rework\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Accessibility depends on the theme, which is chosen first\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Screen readers cannot run until narration is recorded\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun every slide through the six layers in order, fix what each one exposes, and attach a revision trail that a reviewer can inspect. A finished-looking deck with no trail is a result without evidence of the work.\n\nCheck the assembled deck against three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Does the whole deck drive the named audience toward one action, with no off-target slides?\n2. **Evidence check:** For each slide, which of the six layers did you verify, and what did the failing layers expose?\n3. **Revision check:** What gaps did the pipeline catch, and which suggestion did you reject and why?\n\nIf you cannot point to a gap the pipeline caught, you probably skimmed rather than ran it.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating the six layers as independent boxes to tick in any order. They are dependent; running them out of order forces rework, especially checking accessibility or applying a theme before the content is settled.\n\nThe second is believing a polished surface means the pipeline passed. Polish is the last and thinnest layer; it routinely hides a label title, an unverified chart, or a contrast failure underneath. The trail, not the finish, is the evidence.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the pipeline to a slide that passes the early layers but fails a late one, to see why order matters. Slide 5 has a strong purpose (it shows the cost saving the panel cares about) and a real assertion headline (\"Caching saved 40 hours per month\"). Media is fine, narration adds the cause, citation links to the exact log. Then accessibility catches it: the savings are encoded as a green-to-red heat strip with no labels, invisible to a colorblind panelist.\n\nBecause you ran the layers in order, this is a cheap fix at the end rather than a redo. The content is final, so you add value labels to the heat strip once and the check passes for good. Had you \"done accessibility first,\" you would have labeled an earlier draft of the strip and then re-labeled it after the content changed. The walkthrough shows the payoff of the order: late checks land on stable content, and the revision trail records one clean fix instead of three.\n\nOrder the six capstone layers the way the pipeline runs them, where each depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Purpose: name the audience and the single action the deck should drive\", \"Structure: make each title an assertion the evidence proves\", \"Media: confirm each image explains and is verified\", \"Narration: ensure the voice adds reasoning instead of rereading the slide\", \"Citation: verify every source and figure and disclose AI authorship\", \"Accessibility: check contrast, alt text, and not-color-alone on final content\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each of the six layers; on the right, the gap it caught on your deck and the fix. Flag any slide where you skipped a layer. By the end of 05.08, your note should be the backbone of the revision trail you submit.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. Why must the six layers run in this order?\n2. Which layer caught the gap that polish was hiding?\n3. What does the revision trail show that the finished deck cannot?\n4. Which suggestion did you reject, and why did it add no value?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe capstone storyboard is strong when a reviewer can walk the same six layers and see, for each, what you verified and what you fixed, plus a rejected suggestion with its reason. Treat the polished deck as the smallest part of the grade; the trail of decisions is the rest.\n\nA useful note would sound like this: \"Running the pipeline, structure caught a label title on slide 3 ('Results') which I rewrote to 'Cut report time 40 percent,' and accessibility caught a contrast failure on the same slide, which I fixed by darkening the title past 4.5:1. I used BigAL for alt-text wording and disclosed it. I rejected an animated transition suggestion, because it added motion but no meaning and could distract from the headline.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nSuppose the final deliverable changes from a live pitch to a self-running file a recruiter opens alone. Re-run the same six layers and watch which results shift: purpose holds, structure holds, but narration must move into the slides (no live voice), and accessibility gets stricter (no presenter to clarify). The pipeline is the transferable asset; the deck it produces changes with the constraint. If you can re-derive the deck by re-running the layers rather than restarting, you have learned the studio as a process, not a one-time assignment.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this capstone pulls together. See how running the full six-layer pipeline feeds evidence-based revision of the storyboard:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_module_5_presentation_studio\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-08-module-5-presentation-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of why the six layers run in a fixed order.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a polished deck with no trail against one whose trail shows the gaps each layer caught.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the pipeline to a new deliverable and predict which layers shift.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where one layer caught a gap polish was hiding.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the presentation storyboard with audience constraint and slide-level evidence for CAT 100 05.08. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_5_presentation_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm every slide passed all six layers in order, the deck drives one audience action, and your revision trail records the gaps each layer caught plus one rejected suggestion and why.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_module_5_presentation_studio", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_module_5_presentation_studio", "cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"], "stem": "Your Module 5 capstone deck is due. Which submission shows you carried the whole pipeline, not just polish?", "options": ["A deck with a slick theme and animations but no stated purpose and a failed contrast check.", "A deck that names its audience and purpose, uses assertion headlines, has one verified explanatory chart with a source line, narration that adds reasoning, and passes contrast and alt-text checks.", "Twelve unedited AI-generated slides submitted as is.", "A single resume slide with decorative fonts."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The strong capstone integrates every Module 5 layer (purpose, structure, media, narration, citation, accessibility) before polish.", "difficulty": "easy", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_skip_layers"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which note gives BigAL a useful capstone-integration problem?", "options": ["My capstone is done.", "My deck has purpose, structure, and citations, but slide 3's narration just rereads the chart and slide 5 fails contrast. Which do I fix first for the panel?", "Help with my project.", "I finished it."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "The note names specific pipeline gaps (narration and contrast) and asks for prioritized feedback for the audience.", "difficulty": "medium", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_no_revision_trail"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "What is a defensible role for AI in the capstone?", "options": ["Have AI build and finalize the entire deck for you.", "Use AI for bounded moves (draft alt text, critique the headline arc), then make and document every integration decision yourself.", "Let AI invent the data and citations so the deck looks complete.", "Use AI only to apply a theme and submit."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI assists single bounded moves; the capstone must show your own integrated judgment and a revision trail.", "difficulty": "hard", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_ai_does_capstone"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_5_8_module_5_presentation_studio_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which capstone record best supports a fair review of your work?", "options": ["Only the final .pptx file.", "The before/after of one slide, the issue you noticed, the AI or peer support used, and one suggestion you rejected with the reason.", "Only the submission timestamp.", "Only 